{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Since the onset of Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID19), SARS-CoV-2 infection has been circulating globally, with approximately six peak waves of outbreaks worldwide before May 26, 2023... The fact that SARS-CoV-2 is still mutating indicates that it remains a global health threat. The WHO study published in Nature [1, 3] also suggested that accurately tracking SARS-CoV-2 and its impact had been challenging.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the global context of the COVID-19 pandemic, the ongoing threat of mutations, and the difficulty in tracking the virus, establishing the current state of knowledge and uncertainties."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "In addition, the advantage of this study was that compared with the previous meta-analysis of the SARSCoV-2 reinfection rate, first, to avoid the heterogeneous impact of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection definition on SARSCoV-2 reinfection rates, so this study defined reinfection and included only literature with reinfection intervals greater than 90 days. Secondly, considering the heterogeneity of reinfection rates of different variants, we constructed a meta-regression of time-varying reinfection rates and compared it with the temporal spline regression without considering heterogeneity to explore the temporal trends of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rates, which could provide a basis for subsequent research on prediction of SARS-CoV-2 infection, and could guide significance for the future policy formulation of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection.",
      "reason": "The authors explain the need for the review by highlighting limitations in previous studies (heterogeneity in definitions) and the specific methodological improvements (meta-regression, spline regression) they offer to better understand reinfection trends and guide policy."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "In addition, the advantage of this study was that compared with the previous meta-analysis of the SARSCoV-2 reinfection rate, first, to avoid the heterogeneous impact of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection definition on SARSCoV-2 reinfection rates, so this study defined reinfection and included only literature with reinfection intervals greater than 90 days.",
      "reason": "The authors explicitly acknowledge the existence of previous meta-analyses and explain the need for their current review by contrasting their methodology (specifically regarding reinfection definitions) with those previous studies."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is a systematic review of observational studies (cohort, case-control, cross-sectional) regarding reinfection rates, not a review of intervention effects."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is not a review of complex interventions or delivery contexts; it is an epidemiological meta-analysis of reinfection rates."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Therefore, the aim of this study was to conduct a meta-analysis to describe and summarize the current reinfection rates in different regions and among diverse populations. Furthermore, the study sought to examine trends in reinfection rates over time, so as to provide reference for the development of global unified reinfection standards and reinfection measures in the future.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states the study's objectives regarding the meta-analysis of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rates and the examination of trends over time."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is an observational study (meta-analysis of reinfection rates) rather than a review of intervention effects, so PICO comparisons are not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The inclusion criteria were: (1) Conformed to research questions... (2) The study design included cohort study, case-control study, and descriptive study; (3) The study fited our working definition of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection... reinfection working definition in this study was based on a positive laboratory result at least 90 days after laboratory confirmation of primary infection... (4) Original research. The exclusion criteria were: (1) The sample size was less than 20; (2) Belonged to one of the following types of studies: editorial, case report, case series study, systematic review, meta-analysis, animal experiment, news report; (3) Secondary reporting or articles on repeated studies of the same population; (4) Grey literature",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly lists inclusion and exclusion criteria, including study designs (cohort, case-control, descriptive), sample size requirements, and a specific working definition for reinfection."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "To avoid language bias, the search was not limited to any language. We conducted preliminary screening based on the English title and English abstract provided by nonEnglish articles. Further, in the screening process, if the included article was neither in English nor in Chinese and did not provide an English abstract or full text, we used an online translator (Google Translate) for translation.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies the criteria regarding language (no language limit) and the handling of non-English articles."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "One of them reported only the reinfection rate, but not the associated number of first infections as well as the number of reinfections... Therefore, we excluded the above literature with the number of unreported infections",
      "reason": "The manuscript distinguishes between excluding studies due to missing data (number of first infections/reinfections) versus other reasons, fulfilling the requirement to clarify why studies were excluded."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Subgroup analyses were performed by meta-regression to fit stratified rates of reinfection based on variables such as variant and country, and we also used spline regression to fit time-varying reinfection rates",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies how studies were grouped for synthesis (subgroup analyses by age, variant, country, study population, and study type) and links these to the objectives (e.g., fitting time-varying rates)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The exclusion criteria were: (1) The sample size was less than 20; ... (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "The manuscript provides rationales for restrictions, such as excluding studies with sample sizes less than 20 and excluding grey literature to maintain data quality/control."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

